public_system.narrative:
Australia's public system rests on Medicare, a universal insurance program financed from general tax revenue and a levy, which covers citizens, permanent residents and people from reciprocal-agreement countries, pays the full cost of public hospital services and part of the cost of other services, and subsidizes most medicines through the Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme.{{1}} The Commonwealth funds care through the Medicare Benefits Schedule and the Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme, and the states own and manage the public hospitals.{{1}}

In 2023-24 there were 704 public hospitals with 67,280 available beds, or 2.5 per 1,000 people, and total recurrent spending of A$103.1 billion, 94 percent of it funded by governments.{{2}} Public hospitals recorded about 7.5 million separations that year and 9,094,312 emergency department presentations in 2024-25.{{2}}

On 30 January 2026 National Cabinet reached an agreement under which the Commonwealth will provide A$25 billion in additional public hospital funding.{{3}} The 2026-27 Budget provides A$220.3 billion over five years to the states and territories for public hospital services and the 2026-2031 addendum.{{4}} In primary care, bulk billing incentives were extended to all Australians from 1 November 2025, and the government projects that 9 in 10 GP visits will be bulk billed by 2030.{{5}}{{6}}

education.narrative:
Medical education runs through public and private universities: students complete an Australian Medical Council-accredited degree, followed by an internship and residency.{{1}} The government is adding Commonwealth supported medical places: universities can apply for an additional 100 places from 2026, rising to 150 a year from 2028, with a focus on training more GPs, and the government said it would introduce demand driven places for First Nations medical students from 2026.{{2}}

After graduation, doctors train through the specialist colleges. The Commonwealth-funded Australian General Practice Training Program, for example, offers 3 to 4 years of full-time training.{{3}} The 2024 Medical Training Survey had 24,812 respondents, more than half of doctors in training, and 62 percent of trainees said the financial cost of their college training program had led to stress.{{7}}

Doctors who trained overseas generally follow a standard pathway: they pass the Australian Medical Council examinations to earn the AMC certificate, then hold provisional registration while completing approved supervised practice before general registration.{{5}} The Medical Board advises that Australian graduates have priority for accredited internship positions.{{5}} Specialists with approved qualifications from the United Kingdom, New Zealand and Ireland can instead gain specialist registration through an expedited pathway without college assessment, and must then complete six months of supervised practice.{{4}}{{6}}
